## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design real-time, scalable guardrails spanning telemetry, model routing, and incident response in multi-tenant LLM gateways, a common production pain point.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming telemetry and per-tenant baselines
- Anomaly scoring combining statistical and rule-based signals
- Remediation policies: throttle, safe-model routing, quarantine
- Auditability and drift handling across tenants
- Data locality and privacy impacts

## Code Example
```javascript
function score(latency, tokens, entropy, baseline) {
  const zLat = (latency - baseline.latency.mean) / baseline.latency.std;
  const zTok = (tokens - baseline.tokens.mean) / baseline.tokens.std;
  const zEnt = (entropy - baseline.entropy.mean) / baseline.entropy.std;
  return Math.max(0, Math.abs(zLat) + Math.abs(zTok) + Math.abs(zEnt));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you tune thresholds to minimize false positives for bursts?
- How would you validate end-to-end remediation without impacting real users?

## Why This Is Asked
This angle probes privacy-by-design, auditing, and latency in a realistic multi-tenant LLM-ops path.

## Key Concepts
- PII detection: regex + lightweight ML, handling false positives
- Deterministic redaction for audit trails; cryptographic signing if possible
- Per-tenant KMS-bound logging and data locality
- Latency strategies: streaming/partial redact, caching
- Testing: cross-tenant isolation and deterministic outputs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo redact for PII in prompts
function redactPII(input, patterns, tenantKey) {
  // apply regex-based masking, then sign
  const redacted = patterns.reduce((acc, p) => acc.replace(p, '[REDACTED]'), input)
  return { text: redacted, auditSig: sign(redacted, tenantKey) }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify redaction never leaks metadata in outputs?
- How would you handle redaction altering meaning or rendering prompts invalid?

